<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>Baraka says: <q>The graph of <m>y = \sin({2}x + {60}^\circ)</m> is the graph of <m>y = \sin {2}x</m> translated <m>{60}^\circ</m> to the left.</q></p><p>(a) Which statement is correct?</p><div><ol marker="(1)">
<li>Baraka is right: adding 60<m>^\circ</m> inside the bracket moves the graph 60<m>^\circ</m> to the left.</li>
<li>Baraka is wrong: <m>\sin(2x + 60^\circ) = \sin 2(x + 30^\circ)</m>, so the graph moves 30<m>^\circ</m> to the left.</li>
<li>Baraka is wrong: the plus sign means the graph moves to the right.</li>
<li>Baraka is wrong: adding an angle inside the bracket changes the period of the wave.</li>
</ol>  </div><p>(b) Find the <m>x</m>-coordinate of the first maximum point of <m>y = \sin({2}x + {60}^\circ)</m> with <m>x \gt 0</m>.</p><div><fillin characters="6" name="ans2"/> <m>^\circ</m>  </div></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>(a) <m>{2}x + {60}^\circ = {2}(x + {30}^\circ)</m>, so <m>y = \sin({2}x + {60}^\circ)</m> is <m>y = \sin {2}x</m> with <m>x</m> replaced by <m>x + {30}^\circ</m>: a translation of <m>{30}^\circ</m> to the left, not <m>{60}^\circ</m>. The graph moves the opposite way to the sign, by the angle divided by <m>{2}</m>. So the correct statement is the one that gives a shift of <m>{30}^\circ</m> to the left.</p><p>(b) Sine is greatest when its angle is <m>90^\circ</m>: <m>{2}x + {60} = 90</m>, so <m>x = \dfrac{90 - {60}}{{2}} = {15}^\circ</m>. Check: the maximum of <m>y = \sin {2}x</m> is at <m>{45}^\circ</m>, and <m>{45}^\circ - {30}^\circ = {15}^\circ</m>.</p></p></solution>
    <answer><p><m>(2)</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>15</m></p></answer>
</stack-static>